lesson:
# Trie (Prefix Tree)

A trie stores words along **paths of characters**. Words sharing a prefix share the nodes for that prefix, so every operation costs only the length of the word — never the size of the dictionary.

```java
class Node { Node[] ch = new Node[26]; boolean end; }
Node root = new Node();
void insert(String w){
    Node n = root;
    for (char c : w.toCharArray()) {
        int i = c - 'a';
        if (n.ch[i] == null) n.ch[i] = new Node();  // create child
        n = n.ch[i];
    }
    n.end = true;                                    // mark word end
}
```

## search vs startsWith
| Query | Succeeds when |
|---|---|
| `search(w)` | the path exists **and** the last node has `end == true` |
| `startsWith(p)` | the path exists (end flag irrelevant) |

## When to reach for this
Signals: *'prefix'*, *'autocomplete / suggestions'*, *'dictionary of words'*, *'words with wildcard'*, and 'maximum XOR' (a binary trie of bits).

## Simulated solve — insert 'app', 'apple', search 'app'
| step | path |
|---|---|
| insert app | root-a-p-p (p.end=true) |
| insert apple | reuse a-p-p, add l-e (e.end=true) |
| search app | walk a-p-p, end==true → **true** |
| search ap | walk a-p, end==false → **false** |

